  "body": {
    "id": "lem:corrected-confidence-coverage",
    "kind": "lemma",
    "statement": "For every fixed known \\((k,m)\\) and \\(\\alpha\\in(0,1)\\), the corrected inverted set obeys\n\\[\n\\inf_{\\theta\\in\\mathcal M_{n,k,m}}\n\\Pr_\\theta\\{[h_\\theta]\\in\\mathcal C_N(\\alpha)\\}\n\\ge1-\\alpha-o(1).\n\\]",
    "free_symbols": [
      "n",
      "k",
      "m",
      "alpha",
      "confidence_set",
      "N",
      "theta",
      "model_class",
      "encoder"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:iid-environment-sampling",
      "def:quotient-confidence-set",
      "lem:certified-spectral-law-recovery"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "Let\n\\[\n \\mathcal S_N(\\theta)=\n \\left\\{\\Delta(\\mathbb P_\\theta,\\widehat{\\mathbb P}_L)\n \\leq c_\\alpha\\rho_N\\right\\},\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\Delta\\) is the fixed-label Euclidean sum of the Hellinger distances over the finite environment set \\(\\mathcal A\\).  The calibration in Definition \\(\\mathrm{def:quotient\\text{-}confidence\\text{-}set}\\), under Assumption \\(\\mathrm{ass:iid\\text{-}environment\\text{-}sampling}\\), states\n\\[\n \\inf_{\\theta\\in\\mathcal M_{n,k,m}}\n \\Pr_\\theta\\{\\mathcal S_N(\\theta)\\}\n \\geq1-\\alpha-o(1).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\nThe deterministic minimum-distance comparison proved in Lemma \\(\\mathrm{lem:certified\\text{-}spectral\\text{-}law\\text{-}recovery}\\) is\n\\[\n D_H(\\theta,\\widehat\\theta_N)\n \\leq2\\Delta(\\mathbb P_\\theta,\\widehat{\\mathbb P}_L)+2\\delta_N.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nIndeed, (2) uses a \\(\\delta_N\\)-net representative, the defining \\(\\delta_N\\)-approximate minimum-distance inequality, and two applications of the finite-family Hellinger triangle inequality; closure of the net under common node permutations converts the net approximation to the fixed labeling used by the objective.\n\nOn \\(\\mathcal S_N(\\theta)\\), equations (1)--(2) imply\n\\[\n D_H(\\theta,\\widehat\\theta_N)\n \\leq2c_\\alpha\\rho_N+2\\delta_N.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nThe true model \\(\\theta\\) is a member of \\(\\mathcal M_{n,k,m}(c_0,C_0,K,a_0,J,\\Delta_0)\\).  Therefore (3) makes \\(\\theta\\) an admissible representing model in Definition \\(\\mathrm{def:quotient\\text{-}confidence\\text{-}set}\\), and that definition places its encoder class \\([h_\\theta]\\) in \\(\\mathcal C_N(\\alpha)\\).  Thus\n\\[\n \\Pr_\\theta\\{[h_\\theta]\\in\\mathcal C_N(\\alpha)\\}\n \\geq\\Pr_\\theta\\{\\mathcal S_N(\\theta)\\}.\n\\]\nTaking the infimum over \\(\\theta\\) and applying (1) proves the claimed uniform coverage.  This argument uses the quotient only to report the set element; it does not infer quotient equivalence from the vanishing of \\(d_{\\mathrm{rep}}\\).",
